\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {1}{\ignorespaces Model architecture and information flow through one derivative evaluation. The fast subsystem (blue) is algebraic and is resolved once per evaluation: environmental forcing S1 drives the stomatal supply function S2 and the photosynthetic demand function S3, which are closed against each other in S4 as a quadratic in the intercellular CO$_2$ concentration and solved in closed form. The resulting net assimilation is converted to the model carbon input $P_H$. The slow subsystem (orange) comprises the eleven integrated states: the carbohydrate economy S5, nutrient acquisition with its explicit costs S5b, and leaf and root biomass S6. Growth returns to the carbohydrate economy through sink activation and through leaf area, which closes the only feedback loop in the architecture. Arrows show the direction of information flow, not stoichiometric mass transfer; the carbon budget is stated in Section\nobreakspace  {}\ref  {sec:carbon_closure}.}}{6}{figure.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:overview}{{1}{6}{Model architecture and information flow through one derivative evaluation. The fast subsystem (blue) is algebraic and is resolved once per evaluation: environmental forcing S1 drives the stomatal supply function S2 and the photosynthetic demand function S3, which are closed against each other in S4 as a quadratic in the intercellular CO$_2$ concentration and solved in closed form. The resulting net assimilation is converted to the model carbon input $P_H$. The slow subsystem (orange) comprises the eleven integrated states: the carbohydrate economy S5, nutrient acquisition with its explicit costs S5b, and leaf and root biomass S6. Growth returns to the carbohydrate economy through sink activation and through leaf area, which closes the only feedback loop in the architecture. Arrows show the direction of information flow, not stoichiometric mass transfer; the carbon budget is stated in Section~\ref {sec:carbon_closure}}{figure.1}{}}
